          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "Antibiotics are helpful for bacterial infections. They do not work against viruses.\n\nMany common illnesses, such as colds, most sore throats, and the flu, are caused by viruses. For these conditions, antibiotics will not shorten the illness or prevent complications. Using antibiotics when they are not needed can cause side effects like allergic reactions or digestive problems. It also contributes to antibiotic resistance, making these medicines less effective for serious bacterial infections in the future (guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, condition-upper-respiratory-infection, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals).\n\nWhen there is a bacterial infection, antibiotics can be very important. For example, they are used for bacterial complications of the flu (guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals) or for bacterial urinary tract infections (guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment).\n\nYour doctor can determine if your illness is likely bacterial or viral and if antibiotics are appropriate.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
